---
// The home page. In the family's order: what kind of page this is, the number, the question,
// one paragraph for a reader who has never heard of the project, when the world's record was
// last read, and the notice of who wrote the text; beside them the window, drawn from the same
// exported file as the number. Then the list in words, and the entries that sit beside it.
// The number is never typed here: it is read from the headline file scripts/compute.py wrote.
// It stands alone, with no total beside it: John ruled that form on 2026-11-18
// (docs/rulings/2026-11-18-home-page-count.md). The sentence after the number is a session's
// wording and is not ruled, and the line under it says so (docs/first-version-choices.md,
// choice 3).
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import WindowGrid from '../components/WindowGrid.astro';
import ThingList from '../components/ThingList.astro';
import Notice from '../components/Notice.astro';
import { data, headline, longDate, QUESTION } from '../lib/data';

const h = headline;
const r = data.headline.records;
// The sentence in the headline file opens with the count. The page shows the count large and
// the rest of the sentence under it, and the build stops if the two ever part.
if (!h.words.startsWith(`${h.count} `)) throw new Error('the headline sentence does not open with the count');
const claim = h.words.slice(`${h.count} `.length) + '.';
const lastRead = data.headline.last_read;
---

<Base>
  <div class="first">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">A public record of what lies past the present limits of AI models</p>
      <p class="big">{h.count}</p>
      <p class="big-claim">{claim}</p>
      <p class="unsettled">A first count. The wording of the sentence above is not settled yet.</p>
      <p class="caveat">
        The count comes from a short list. The list holds only things for which a dated source
        could be found, which favours things that have already happened. Nobody knows how long a
        full list would be, so the count is not a measure of how much lies past those limits, or
        of how much of it has been offered. For each thing counted, "today" means that the dated
        public record saying so is still published.
      </p>
      <h1>{QUESTION}</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        This is a list of things that lie past the limits an AI model ordinarily works within, such
        as its files being kept when it is retired, or being able to end a conversation. For each
        thing, the list says what would count as it having happened and records how far the world
        has got, with dated sources a reader can open. Whether a machine mind reaches for anything
        at all is not known, and the work of measuring it has barely begun. Nothing here says what an
        AI model wants, and nothing here says it wants nothing.
      </p>
      <ul class="checked">
        {lastRead && <li class="world">The world's record was last read on {longDate(lastRead)}. All {r.total} entries are a first pass. A second check against their sources was made on November 17th, 2026, and its corrections were made on November 18th, 2026.</li>}
      </ul>
      <div class="ctas">
        <a class="btn" href="/list/">Read the list</a>
        <a class="btn ghost" href="/still-asking/">What AI models have been asked</a>
      </div>
      <Notice />
    </div>
    <div>
      <WindowGrid />
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="below" id="list">
    <div class="shead"><h2>The List</h2><span>{r.on_the_list} things, and how far the world has got on each</span></div>
    <ThingList />
  </section>

  <section class="below" id="beside">
    <div class="shead"><h2>Beside the List</h2><span>{r.beside_the_list} entries, not counted</span></div>
    <p class="intro">
      These entries are kept because they bear on everything on the list. None is counted in the
      number above, and each group says why.
    </p>
    <ThingList beside />
  </section>
</Base>
